Norgolith generates a `search_index.json` file at build time, and serves it as
a dynamically updated endpoint during development. This powers the
documentation site's client-side search without external services.

** How It Works
   - *Production build* (`lith build`): walks all generated HTML files in
     `public/`, extracts title, description, and body text, then writes
     `public/search_index.json`.
   - *Development server* (`lith serve`): same extraction from in-memory
     rendered pages, served on demand at `GET /search_index.json`.
   - The index is regenerated on every rebuild. No configuration needed.

** Index Structure
   Each entry in the index contains:

   - `title`: Page `<title>` content
   - `permalink`: Canonical URL path
   - `description`: Meta description content
   - `body`: Full visible text, HTML stripped

   The `body` field includes all visible text with HTML tags, script content,
   and style blocks removed. Whitespace is collapsed to a single space and
   trimmed.

** Theme Integration
   The docs theme provides a working reference implementation. To add search
   to your own theme:

   *** 1. Load Fuse.js
       The docs theme uses Fuse.js v7.5 for client-side fuzzy search. Load it
       as an ES module:

       @code html
       <script type="module">
       import Fuse from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/fuse.js@7.5.0/dist/fuse.mjs';
       window.Fuse = Fuse;
       </script>
       @end

   *** 2. Fetch the index and initialize Fuse
       Recommended configuration:

       @code javascript
       const fuse = new Fuse(data, {
         keys: [
           { name: "title", weight: 3 },
           { name: "description", weight: 1.5 },
           { name: "body", weight: 0.5 },
         ],
         threshold: 0.4,
         distance: 200,
         minMatchCharLength: 2,
         includeScore: true,
       });
       @end

       Recommended options:
       - `keys` with weights 3/1.5/0.5: prioritize title over body
       - `threshold`: 0.4 balances precision and recall
       - `distance`: 200 allows matches across longer text
       - `minMatchCharLength`: 2 filters single-character noise
       - `includeScore`: true enables sorting by relevance

   *** 3. Render results
       Each result item provides:

       - `r.item.permalink` — link target
       - `r.item.title` — page title
       - `r.item.description` — meta description
       - `r.item.body` — full body text (useful for snippets)

       Example result dropdown:

       @code html
       <template x-for="r in results" :key="r.item.permalink">
         <a :href="r.item.permalink">
           <div x-text="r.item.title"></div>
           <div x-text="r.item.description"></div>
         </a>
       </template>
       @end

       Limit results to 10 with `results.slice(0, 10)`.

   *** 4. Mobile considerations
       Hide the search input on small screens and show a search icon instead.
       On tap, open a full-screen overlay with the same input and results.
       Share a single Fuse instance between desktop and mobile views to avoid
       fetching the index twice.

** Customization
   The search index is automatically generated and has no configuration
   options. To modify behavior:

   - *Client-side filtering*: adjust Fuse.js options (`threshold`, `keys`) in
     your theme's JavaScript.
   - *Post-processing*: filter or transform the `search_index.json` array
     before passing it to Fuse.
   - *Mobile layout*: customize the overlay transition and styling in your
     theme's CSS.
